That's not true. An SkSTArray *is a* SkTArray and so neither is trivially relocatable. SkTArray has `fOwnMemory` to indicate internally at runtime if the pointer is internal or not. While we normally think of `fOwnMemory` only being set to `false` through one of the protected constructors, it can also happen through use of swap or the move constructor. Note that SkSTArray is a SkTArray and as such an SkSTArray& can be passed to an SkTArray& parameter. The reality is that SkTArray isn't generally trivially relocatable as it is. If we wanted it to be we would need to separate it from SkSTArray first.

```
void doit(SkCanvas* canvas, SkTArray<int>&& a) {
    SkTArray b(std::move(a));
    // do stuff with b, which has an internal pointer
}

void main() {
    SkSTArray<5, int> a;
    a.push_back(1);
    doit(canvas, std::move(a));
}
```

