Hmmm, yes a matrix wrapper + (path | drawable | bitmap) is a general thing we will want to do. At the moment we do need a drawable per size because we don't currently have a "generic strike" concept, though we're eventually going to need something like it. It isn't possible in the general case since glyph paths and even the paths inside the drawable may be size specific (hinted). Every implementation at the moment needs a drawable wrapper for some reason anyway.

1) In general one doesn't want to call any of the glyph setters in a way that will take up space. Setting the drawable in generateMetrics will set the drawable, but the strike won't know how much space it may take up and won't include it in the cache size. It's fine to set no-paths on the glyph because they don't really take up any additional space.

2) I'm not entirely sure what you mean here. In the image path the glyph bounds are calculated based on what will be drawn. The only thing the glyph bounds state is that the glyph won't draw outside those bounds. The glyph image is "implicitly clipped" to the glyph bounds but the idea is this never actually clips anything (unless potentially the cached information in the font is incorrect). In the same way we clip to the drawable bounds because that's what the drawable said its bounds were, so we clip to that to ensure it doesn't draw outside that area. The glyph bounds generated should always match what will be used to draw (and may not reflect other possible ways the glyph may be drawn if the user were to get the path or drawable or whatnot directly). But it's fine to let the bounds be a bit bigger (round them out) since the only thing they indicate is that the drawing won't go outside that.

