Consider when assumedAlignment <= maxAlignment. Align the requestedSize up to assumedAlignment (since assumedAlignment <= maxAlignment the Block cannot start on any of these locations, so return them to be useful). Now the Block will start at one of the following (maxAlignment/assumedAlignment) sized chunk boundaries which follow. There will need to be (maxAlignment/assumedAlignment - 1) of these to ensure that one of them will be at maxAlignment (which means adding maxAlignment - assumedAlignment). Then the blockSize needs to be added to that, so that if the Block ends up on the last one of these that it will have enough space.

When assumedAlignment >= maxAlignment the memory will already sufficiently aligned, so just align the requestedSize up to maxAlignment and add the blockSize.

So I think this works out to

    if (assumedAlignment < maxAlignment) {
        minimumSize = alignUp(requestedSize, assumedAlignment)
                    + maxAlignment - assumedAlignment
                    + blockSize;
    } else {
        minimumSize = alignUp(requestedSize, maxAlignment)
                    + blockSize;
    }

Note this is equivalent to

    minAlignment = std::min(assumedAlignment, maxAlignment);
    minimumSize = alignUp(requestedSize, minAlignment)
                + maxAlignment - minAlignment
                + blockSize;

Reasoning this way I don't have to worry about whether or not blockSize will fit at the maxAlignment found.

